{"baseRefOid":"2d799c28f450a2d83a5bedf2c07699cd32fb3c7a","body":"## Motivation\n\n`ServerArgs.resolved_dict()` projects every field verbatim, and it is the projection the whole-object readbacks spread:\n\n- `entrypoints/engine.py:265` and `:1098` — the `server_args=` line logged at startup\n- `entrypoints/http_server.py:830` — `/server_info` and its `/get_server_info` alias\n- `entrypoints/grpc_bridge.py:427` — the gRPC twin\n- `entrypoints/engine.py:1364` — the in-process `Engine.get_server_info`\n- `managers/tokenizer_manager.py:2060` — the crash-dump config snapshot\n\nThree fields on that record are credentials, and all three were published in the clear. Reproduced with sentinel values:\n\n```\napi_key              = 'SENTINEL-api-key-2b7f'\nadmin_api_key        = 'SENTINEL-admin-api-key-9c31'\nssl_keyfile_password = 'SENTINEL-ssl-keyfile-password-4e08'\n```\n\nThe startup line therefore carries the keys into whatever aggregates the engine's stdout, where they outlive the process.\n\n**This is also a privilege escalation, not only a disclosure.** `/server_info` carries no `@auth_level` decorator, so `decide_request_auth` serves it at `AuthLevel.NORMAL` — when `api_key` is configured, that key alone is sufficient. `admin_api_key` gates the `ADMIN_FORCE` endpoints, which the same function explicitly refuses to unlock with `api_key`. A caller holding only the low-privilege key could read `/server_info`, recover `admin_api_key` from the body, and replay it. Verified end to end against `/clear_hicache_storage_backend`: accepted before this change, refused after.\n\nReported as #37457. That issue names `api_key` and `admin_api_key`; `ssl_keyfile_password` sits on the same record and travels the same path, so it is fixed here too.\n\n## Modifications\n\n- **`arg_groups/arg_utils.py`** — add `Arg(secret=True)` and a `secret_fields(cls)` reader, mirroring the existing `resolvable=True` / `resolvable_fields()` pair. `REDACTED = \"<redacted>\"` lives here too, in the module that imports nothing from `sglang`, so `runtime_context` can read it without a cycle.\n- **`server_args.py`** — mark `api_key`, `admin_api_key`, `ssl_keyfile_password`. Help text is unchanged; the bare-string annotations become `Arg(help=...)`.\n- **`arg_groups/overrides.py`** — `resolution_projection` publishes `REDACTED` for a marked field. This is the single chokepoint all six publication sites above go through, so one guard covers them all.\n- **`runtime_context.py`** — `resolved_server_args_dict` re-applies the redaction after its overlay. `RuntimeContext.override()` accepts any field carrying an `NS` namespace, `api_key` included, and the overlay writes raw values on top of the projected base; redacting only the base would let a runtime override put a credential straight back into the readback.\n\nThe record itself is untouched, so the auth middleware still reads real values off the fields.\n\nAn unset credential still projects as `None` rather than the marker. Whether a key is configured is operational information, and it is already observable from outside — an unauthenticated request is answered rather than refused — so the marker would only make an absent key look like a present one.\n\n### A deliberate deviation from the issue\n\n#37457 asks for a strict positive allowlist over every `ServerArgs` field, so that unknown future fields are redacted by default. I did not implement that, and want to flag the choice rather than bury it.\n\n`ServerArgs` has 488 fields. An enumerated allowlist means every new server argument is invisible in `/server_info` until someone adds it to the list, which is a standing tax on unrelated PRs and a silent-breakage risk for the monitoring tools and KV-aware routers that scrape the endpoint.\n\n`TestNewCredentialFieldsMustBeClassified` buys the same protection at the point where it matters. It fails when a field whose name contains a credential word is neither marked `secret=True` nor listed as reviewed-public, so a new `--*-key` cannot reach the projection without a human deciding what it is — while the other 480-odd fields need no bookkeeping. Six existing fields are listed as reviewed-public; all six are the LLM sense of \"token\" (`bucket_time_to_first_token`, `speculative_token_map`, and similar).\n\nHappy to switch to the full allowlist if maintainers prefer the stricter contract.\n\n## Tests\n\nNew `test/registered/unit/server_args/test_credential_redaction.py` (11 cases) covers the projection, the escalation, the overlay path, that the record keeps real values, and that nothing beyond the three marked fields is redacted. New `TestServerInfoRedactsCredentials` in `test/registered/unit/entrypoints/test_server_info.py` pins the same property at the endpoint.\n\nPer `.claude/rules/unit-test-admission.md`, the behavioral cases were verified to fail on pre-fix code: reverting only the two behavior hunks (keeping the plumbing so imports resolve) produced 12 failures; restoring them gives 11/11.\n\nBoth files run green against a full `uv pip install -e \"python[dev]\"` environment (torch 2.13.0+cu130, `sgl_kernel` imported):\n\n```\n41 passed, 15 warnings, 30 subtests passed in 110.30s\n```\n\nThat is the 11 new cases plus all 30 pre-existing `test_server_info.py` cases — including `test_every_server_args_field_appears_in_response`, which confirms redaction replaces values without dropping keys. No GPU is involved; this matches what `base-a-test-cpu` does on `ubuntu-latest`.\n\n## Accuracy Tests\n\nNot applicable — no kernel or model forward code is touched.\n\n## Speed Tests and Profiling\n\nNot applicable. `secret_fields()` is `functools.lru_cache`d per class, and the projection gains one set-membership test per field on a diagnostic path that is not on any request hot path.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Format your code according to the [Format code with pre-commit](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#format-code-with-pre-commit). (ran the pinned `isort==7.0.0`, `black==26.1.0`, `ruff==0.15.1` with the hook's exact `--select`; all no-ops)\n- [x] Add unit tests according to the [Run and add unit tests](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#run-and-add-unit-tests).\n- [ ] Update documentation according to [Write documentations](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#write-documentations). (no user-facing behavior change beyond the redacted diagnostic output)\n- [ ] Provide accuracy and speed benchmark results — not applicable, see above.\n- [x] Follow the SGLang code style [guidance](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#code-style-guidance).\n\n<!-- pr-states:start -->\n---\n### CI States\n\nLatest PR Test (Base): <!-- slot:pr-test:start -->:x: [Run #33683024706](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/33683024706)<!-- slot:pr-test:end -->\nLatest PR Test (Extra): <!-- slot:pr-test-extra:start -->:x: [Run #33683024486](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/33683024486)<!-- slot:pr-test-extra:end -->\nLatest PR Test (AMD ROCm 7.2): <!-- slot:pr-test-amd-rocm720:start -->:x: [Run #33683024754](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/33683024754)<!-- slot:pr-test-amd-rocm720:end -->\n<!-- pr-states:end -->","commits":[{"authoredDate":"2026-09-02T00:21:06Z","authors":[{"email":"107228310+koladefaj@users.noreply.github.com","id":"U_kgDOBmQslg","login":"koladefaj","name":"Kolade Fajimi"}],"committedDate":"2026-09-02T00:22:58Z","messageBody":"Fixes #37457","messageHeadline":"[Bugfix] Redact credentials from the server-args diagnostic projection","oid":"84b3db06b25dabdbfd79b96cf5ec1616c57823fe"},{"authoredDate":"2026-09-02T06:45:32Z","authors":[{"email":"107228310+koladefaj@users.noreply.github.com","id":"U_kgDOBmQslg","login":"koladefaj","name":"Kolade Fajimi"}],"committedDate":"2026-09-02T06:45:32Z","messageBody":"","messageHeadline":"Merge branch 'main' into fix/redact-credentials-in-server-info","oid":"70fabb4d0eb00ff867f